Preserving the commercial
value of mail, as volume
naturally declines

Delivering a viable, mail service
into the future
Twenty years ago, we delivered more than
1 billion letters annually. In FY25 we delivered
~158 million. Annual letter volumes are forecast
to continue declining, we’re forecasting
~110 million letters a year by 2028.
We will continue to reshape our mail service in
response to declining volume, in a way that still
provides a dependable service to New Zealand.
In 2023, we consulted with our people on our
proposed long-term strategic direction for
mail. In 2024, after confirming our direction to
ultimately process and deliver mail through the
parcel network, our work focused on preparing
for an eventual move to a single mail and parcel
urban network. Including working with our
people to prepare for this.
“We need to deliver on our mail and retail
network obligations, while responding
to natural decline,” says Matt. “Bringing
our people, our customers and the public
with us on the journey is our priority, by
appropriately engaging with them around
any potential changes.
“We will be open and transparent as we
manage any changes.”
General Manager – Mail, Matt Geor, says mail
will always be a part of our DNA. “We want to
safeguard what’s uniquely NZ Post by doing
this in the most commercially sustainable
and fit-for-purpose way, reflecting Kiwis’
changing needs around mail.”
We’re tackling mail challenges in several ways,
including long-term network planning, pricing,
network optimisation, operational changes
and other cost-out activities. A review of the
Deed of Understanding with the Government
has been progressed, and we are hopeful that
this will provide us with flexibility to respond to
changing circumstances in the future. In 2013,
when the Deed was last reviewed, New Zealand
households received an average of 7.5 letters a
week, compared to an average of 2 letters now.
We are forecasting that this will fall to ~1 item,
or less, per week by 2028.

